THE ONE-BIT FACT: restricting to primes "with enough digits" prunes by a
factor of EXACTLY 2. Primes are top-heavy — 1/ln x barely moves across an
octave (0.1% at 2¹⁰²⁴), so π(x) − π(x/2) ≈ x/(2 ln x) ≈ ½π(x). HALF OF ALL
PRIMES BELOW ANY BOUND LIVE IN THE TOP OCTAVE. Saving measured: 1.0028 at
1024 bits, 1.0014 at 2048, 1.0007 at 4096 → exactly 1 in the limit.

Joins the other cheap constraints, all single digits: mod4 ≈1 bit,
mod16 = 3 bits, size = 1 bit.

